findings.description:
The document treats "the server" as the unit of design and never decomposes it. Line 21 assigns the single process lifecycle, configuration, tick scheduling, entity storage, environment hosting, the client gateway, serialization, and replay recording. Several pieces of mutable state have no declared owner:

- the spatial index: slice 303 initializes it with world bounds, yet the engine is "stateless between ticks" (100-arch line 70). Who holds it, and who rebuilds or invalidates it on spawn or removal?
- the profile registry, which maps profile names to array indices and is shared by entity management and force evaluation;
- seasonal and time state;
- per-client send queues;
- log buffers;
- RNG state.

Slice 304 also refers to a `SimulationManager` (300-slices line 27) that this document never defines. That is the god-object pattern at the design level, and an unnamed coordinator would likely absorb these responsibilities by default.

Required: a component table (owner, state, interface) covering at least TickLoop, EntityStore, EnvironmentHost, ClientGateway, Recorder, and Config, with each piece of mutable state assigned to one owner.

"Logging" covers three things with different durability, latency, and failure requirements:

- operational logs (structured Python logging, overrun warnings);
- simulation recording (replay data, which must not drop and must preserve order);
- metrics ("provide metrics to diagnose the bottleneck," line 88), which have no mechanism, sink, or interface.

Line 65 writes replay records inside the tick. Line 21 puts "logging infrastructure" in scope, but initiative 320 (Logging & Replay) is a separate initiative with format still TBD (initiative plan lines 46–49). This document absorbs 320 without reconciling the two. The "observable by default" goal (line 31) has no concrete surface.

Required: separate the recorder from the logger, name the metrics interface, and either decide the replay format here or defer it explicitly to 320.
